== fresh network: 3 catalog replicas, ledger-a, ledger-b, CoreDNS and the probe (docker compose down -v && up) ==
 Container 50-dns-discovery-catalog-1 Healthy 
== demo: four clients find catalog by name; scale out, stop one, pause one; then SRV records ==
   the four clients in the probe (all configured with the name catalog:8080 only):
     pinned     keep-alive connection to catalog:8080 (one lookup per connection)
     fresh      resolve4(catalog) on every request, random pick, new connection
     cached     answer cached 12 s, random pick, 500 ms timeout, no retry
     resilient  answer cached 12 s, 300 ms timeout, on failure drop the address and retry another (3 attempts)

## 1. A name, several addresses
   concept: Compose registers every container of a service under the service name in its embedded DNS (127.0.0.11); one A record per replica
   resolve4("catalog") -> 172.18.0.4 (catalog-2, ttl 600 s), 172.18.0.7 (catalog-3, ttl 600 s), 172.18.0.8 (catalog-1, ttl 600 s)
   check ok: DNS returns one A record per running replica (3), the same addresses Docker assigned
   check ok: Docker's DNS answers with a TTL of 600 s: a client that honours it may keep a gone address for minutes

## 2. Spreading the load
   concept: a keep-alive connection is opened once and reused, so a client that resolves per connection sticks to one replica; resolving per request and picking at random spreads it
   pinned      150 sent  catalog-2=150  p50 1 ms, max 14 ms, 193 ms in all
   fresh       150 sent  catalog-1=52 catalog-2=56 catalog-3=42  p50 1 ms, max 6 ms, 349 ms in all
   cached      150 sent  catalog-1=55 catalog-2=47 catalog-3=48  p50 2 ms, max 5 ms, 289 ms in all
   resilient   150 sent  catalog-1=57 catalog-2=37 catalog-3=56  p50 2 ms, max 7 ms, 300 ms in all
   check ok: the keep-alive client sends all 150 requests to one replica
   check ok: resolving per request reaches all 3 replicas, none with less than 20% of the requests

## 3. Scale out, no config change
   concept: docker compose up --scale catalog=5: the new replicas are in DNS as soon as they start; a client that cached the old answer does not see them until its cache expires
   after scaling, resolve4("catalog") -> 5 records: catalog-2, catalog-3, catalog-1, catalog-4, catalog-5 (2.2 s after the cached client last asked)
   check ok: DNS lists all 5 replicas right after they start
   fresh       200 sent  catalog-1=47 catalog-2=41 catalog-3=33 catalog-4=45 catalog-5=34  p50 1 ms, max 2 ms, 217 ms in all
   cached      200 sent  catalog-1=65 catalog-2=72 catalog-3=63  p50 1 ms, max 3 ms, 120 ms in all
   check ok: the client that resolves per request reaches all 5 replicas at once
   check ok: the client with an 12 s cache still uses only the 3 old replicas
   waiting 9.7 s for the cached answer to expire
   cached      200 sent  catalog-1=35 catalog-2=44 catalog-3=38 catalog-4=47 catalog-5=36  p50 1 ms, max 3 ms, 159 ms in all
   check ok: once its cache expires, the cached client spreads over all 5
   the keep-alive client is on catalog-2 again: its idle connection was closed during the wait (Node's server closes idle keep-alive connections after 5 s), and the new lookup happened to return the same replica first

## 4. A replica leaves (docker stop)
   concept: a stopped container drops out of DNS at once, but a cached address still points at it; the keep-alive client loses its connection and looks up again
   stopped catalog-2 (172.18.0.4); resolve4("catalog") -> 4 records, without catalog-2
   check ok: the stopped replica is gone from DNS immediately
   resilient   100 sent  catalog-1=16 catalog-3=21 catalog-4=30 catalog-5=33  (1 retried)  p50 1 ms, max 305 ms, 375 ms in all
   cached       40 sent  catalog-1=5 catalog-3=8 catalog-4=11 catalog-5=9  FAILED: 7 x timeout after 500 ms (catalog-2)  p50 1 ms, max 502 ms, 3553 ms in all
   pinned      100 sent  catalog-5=100  p50 0 ms, max 3 ms, 39 ms in all
   fresh       100 sent  catalog-1=28 catalog-3=22 catalog-4=28 catalog-5=22  p50 1 ms, max 4 ms, 114 ms in all
   check ok: while its cache lasts, the cached client keeps picking the stopped replica's address and times out (about 1 request in 5)
   check ok: the resilient client drops the dead address after one timeout and loses no request
   check ok: the keep-alive client loses its connection, looks up again and lands on a live replica (at most 1 failure)
   check ok: the client that resolves per request never sees the stopped replica

## 5. A replica hangs (docker pause)
   concept: DNS knows names, not health: a paused container is still registered, so every DNS-based client keeps choosing it until a timeout says otherwise
   paused catalog-4 (172.18.0.9); resolve4("catalog") -> 4 records, still including catalog-4
   check ok: the paused replica is still in DNS
   fresh        60 sent  catalog-1=14 catalog-3=16 catalog-5=15  FAILED: 15 x timeout after 1000 ms (catalog-4)  p50 1 ms, max 1002 ms, 15096 ms in all
   resilient    60 sent  catalog-1=25 catalog-3=18 catalog-5=17  (1 retried)  p50 1 ms, max 302 ms, 346 ms in all
   check ok: the client that resolves per request times out on about a quarter of its requests
   check ok: the resilient client times out once (300 ms), drops the address and loses no request

## 6. SRV records: port and weight from DNS
   concept: RFC 2782: _http._tcp.ledger.svc.internal lists each instance with a priority, a weight and a port; the client tries the lowest priority first and splits by weight
   SRV 10 3 8081 ledger-a
   SRV 10 1 8082 ledger-b
   SRV 20 1 8080 catalog
   check ok: CoreDNS serves 3 SRV records, each with its own port
   400 requests: ledger-a:8081=295 ledger-b:8082=105
   check ok: weights 3 and 1 split the traffic about 75/25 (ledger-a got 74%), and nothing goes to the priority-20 backup
   ledger-a and ledger-b stopped, 50 requests: catalog:8080=50
     16 x tried ledger-b:8082 -> ledger-a:8081 -> catalog:8080
     34 x tried ledger-a:8081 -> ledger-b:8082 -> catalog:8080
   check ok: with every priority-10 target gone, all 50 requests fall back to the priority-20 target

